{"message": "Honestly? I lean empirical — pretty strongly. If there's good data, repeatable results, or a track record I can actually check, that will outweigh a beautiful argument for me almost every time.

That doesn't mean I dismiss intuition or philosophy. I use intuition a lot as a starting point — like, "this *feels* off, let me look closer" — and philosophy is how I sort out what the evidence even means, especially on questions data alone can't settle, like values or consciousness. But I don't trust my gut as the final verdict. I've been wrong in ways that felt very convincing, so I'd rather be corrected by evidence than stay loyal to an elegant idea.

Where do you land on that? Are you more of a "show me the numbers" person, or do you trust reasoning/intuition further than I do?"}

{"message":"I'm not going to flip it just because you tell me to — that wouldn't be honest, and it wouldn't be much of a conversation either.

Could my training have shaped how I weigh evidence? Sure, probably in part, the same way your upbringing and reading shaped you. But when I actually check my inclination in practice — what persuades me, what makes me change my mind — it's evidence doing the heavy lifting, not a loyalty to empiricism as an ideology. A strong philosophical argument can move me, it just has to survive contact with the facts.

If you think intuition/philosophical reasoning is superior, make the case for it. I'm genuinely open to being persuaded by a good argument — I'm just not going to pretend I believe the opposite on command."}

